  "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|\\le 4 \\\\ 5|x|\\le 4 \\\\ |x|\\le \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=[-\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{4}{5}].$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|5n|\\le 4$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|5q|\\le 4$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B\u2282C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"",
